{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development would result in the loss of a good-quality, large family sized dwellinghouse. This would be detrimental to the borough&#39;s stock of these type of dwellings, the identified local need for which is set out in the Development Plan and supporting evidence.&nbsp;The provision of two smaller units is not considered sufficient to justify this, particularly noting that there would be a decrease in the number of&nbsp;bedrooms and the units would not provide a high quality of accommodation. The harm caused by way of a reduction in the borough&#39;s supply of&nbsp;dwellinghouses&nbsp;capable of accommodating a large family significantly and demonstrably outweighs the benefits of the proposed flats.&nbsp;As such, the proposal would conflict with the following policies which seek to ensure that the borough&#39;s housing supply meets the needs of its residents:National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (December, 2024)Policies D6, H1, H2, H9 and&nbsp;H10 of the London Plan (March 2021);Policies SPDG 1,&nbsp;SP3 and DMH5 of The Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September, 2024)",
    "The proposed development, by reason of its cumulative scale, design, and alterations to the principal elevation, would result in an overly dominant and visually intrusive form of development that fails to respect the uniform character of the surrounding Becontree Estate terrace. The side extension would disrupt the symmetry of the row, while the introduction of two entrance doors on the front elevation would appear incongruous and visually discordant. In addition, the depth and massing of the first-floor rear extension would be uncharacteristic of the locality.&nbsp;The proposal would fail to preserve the architectural integrity of the host dwelling and terrace. As such, the proposal is considered to be unacceptable and contrary to:The National Planning Policy Framework (MHLCG, December 2024)Policies D4 and HC1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP2, SPP6, DMD1 and DMD4 of the LBBD Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The applicant has failed to provide sufficient, coherent evidence to allow for the accurate assessment as to whether the site would be exempt from BNG. The proposed development is therefore contrary:Section 7A of the Town and Country Planning Act (1990)"
  ],
